#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bcd0b79-b591-5a5d-bc14-b364aa6c340b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444099} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8cded8-ec23-5c9b-8c9a-98e1f9d2ef7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444103} {:answer/id #uuid "b10cf290-9394-50d1-819e-a7d17fd1323e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444104} {:answer/id #uuid "b175c960-bed3-5e60-a632-6acb4a7535f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444105} {:answer/id #uuid "b282c837-f2fd-5a48-aa69-2a1b263dcd48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b10cf290-9394-50d1-819e-a7d17fd1323e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444104} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc17c284-d49b-5770-afca-b8873208fda5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65b731fa-e5f1-557a-845a-24f0962ea2c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125112" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x+2)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)$ at $x=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2))(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{1-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-2)=-\\frac{1}{(1-(-2))^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x+2)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{9})}{2}=-\\frac{1}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c811009-4fed-5852-8db2-367cbfc62885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2x+2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444095} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc0dcfd-3f59-5279-8254-acb28a240f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2x+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444101} {:answer/id #uuid "838b01b8-dd55-5bfd-9de8-6678351f50a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2x+8{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444102} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b5619b-9639-5e1b-ae10-f79f6124a398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444109} {:answer/id #uuid "ed478f76-e738-5394-aa55-550c7976990d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c811009-4fed-5852-8db2-367cbfc62885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2x+2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444095} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa06994-52fc-5e1e-8d0d-4755fd6fcc50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce61f109-1d63-597e-a6a4-e3b8e6eb9f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-8155" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-2x}$ about $x=0$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-2x} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2{e}^{-2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{-2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+(-2)x+\\frac{4}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =1-2x+2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
